# shir_nodes/gpu_op.node — a GPU device operation lifted from a shell write.
#
# THE §4.2 STAGE ② "gpu-prep" NODE (docs/BASH-O4.md). A shell program draws
# by writing structured text to a device file (the Plan 9 model MIMEcroft's
# fb0 port is built on):
#
#     echo "$blk_p" > /dev/fb0/blocks
#     echo "swap"   > /dev/fb0/call
#
# This node is that write, lifted out of the shell program and into the
# device boundary, so a backend with a GPU leg can emit a dispatch
# (`_sh_gpu_*`, §4.4) instead of a filesystem write. A backend WITHOUT a GPU
# leg renders the node as the original write — the correctness firewall.
#
# Fields:
#   device        the device root ("/dev/fb0", "/dev/webgl")
#   verb          the operation, i.e. the LAST path segment
#                 ("blocks" | "call" | "depthmask" | "hud" | …)
#   slot          the sub-segments before the verb, joined with '/'
#                 (a uniform's "1f/uCamYaw"; empty for a top-level verb)
#   payload       the written value (the batched draw payload, "swap", …)
#   id            a stable per-program id (sh_gpu_dev_<n>), so an emitted
#                 dispatch site and its SPIR-V blob can be named together
#                 (docs/BASH-O4.md §4.4: `_sh_spv_<id>`)

node GpuOp
tag "GpuOp"
field device: string
field verb: string
field slot: string
field payload: expr
field id: string
# Unstated — the missing component is DEVICE MEMORY AND DISPATCH: buffer allocation and
# transfers, workgroup/thread geometry, synchronisation and async completion. No
# byte-level value contract exists for a dispatch, and nothing here models a device heap.
# Recorded as a named missing model rather than the bare word "effect".
# LAW CANDIDATE: `after dispatch completes, out_buffer = kernel(in_buffer)`. That is the
# KERNEL's property, not the node's: the node enqueues work, and its own observable value
# is a completion handle, so the law would have to quantify over device buffers, transfer
# direction, workgroup/thread geometry and async completion order. None of these can be
# abstracted away without the law becoming vacuous (`out = out`), so the missing component
# is device memory plus dispatch/completion semantics, named rather than called "effect".
spec: none (unstated: missing model — device memory and dispatch: buffers and transfers,
# workgroup geometry, synchronisation, async completion ordering.)
